<h2 id="thesis">The thesis: software construction is pre-"Laws of Coding"</h2>

<p>Picture Europe in the 1660s. Kepler had published two regularities of planetary motion in <em>Astronomia nova</em> (1609) and a third in <em>Harmonice mundi</em> (1619) [T2]. Galileo and Huygens had derived testable conclusions from hypotheses about motion [T1, Sec. 1]. Gunners, millwrights and shipwrights built by rule of thumb and long apprenticeship. The sky had been watched with enormous care for two thousand years, and yet prediction was a patchwork. Newton, by the account of George Smith, "was familiar with seven different approaches to calculating planetary orbits, all at roughly the same accuracy", and calculated positions "were sometimes off by as much as a fourth of the width of the Moon" [T1, Sec. 2]. There were many good tables and no single account of why the tables worked.</p>

<p>Then, in 1687, came the <em>Principia</em>. A handful of axioms of motion and one inverse-square force, and from them trajectories that could be computed in advance, costs that could be reckoned before the cannon was cast, and errors that pointed to new bodies rather than to bad luck. Smith summarizes the change: after the <em>Principia</em>, "physical theory gained primacy over observation for purposes of answering specific questions about the world" [T1, Sec. 1]. Galileo's results for uniform gravity did not disappear; they became a limit case of inverse-square gravity, and the theory went on to explain the planets' deviations from Kepler's rules [T1, Secs. 1 and 2].</p>

<p>The premise, in plain words: <strong>the practice of software construction, including today's AI-assisted coding by large language models, is pre-"Laws of Coding".</strong> Here "the practice of software construction" means the practitioners and systems, human developers and code-generating models alike, that produce code by empirical trial, testing and review rather than by derivation from a formal rule set. That practice today lives in the 1660s. It has careful observation: benchmarks such as pass@k (the chance that at least one of k sampled programs passes the tests) and SWE-bench (a set of real repository issues), both described in Section 3 below. It has craft: prompt wording, retries, and trial and error. Outputs of code-generating models are samples from a probability distribution, so the same request can give a different program tomorrow; when ChatGPT (gpt-3.5-turbo at its default temperature) was asked the same coding question five times, for 47.56% to 75.76% of problems across three datasets there was no test on which all five answers gave the same output, and "setting the temperature to 0 does not guarantee determinism in code generation" (Ouyang et al., 2023, abstract; Section 3 below). The regularities that the human side of the practice does show are, in Lehman's words, ones that "arise from the habits and practices of people and organizations" [T41, Sec. V-A, p. 1069]. Hoare and colleagues named the same gap in 1987: "What then are the laws of programming, which provide the formal basis for the profession of software engineering? Many programmers may be unable to quote even a single law" [T36, p. 673]. Dijkstra had already marked the limit of the empirical method that the practice still relies on: "program testing can be a very effective way to show the presence of bugs, but is hopelessly inadequate for showing their absence" [T37, Argument three]. There is no agreed conserved quantity for a program, no equation of motion that a code generator obeys, and cost is measured after the fact, in tokens and in electricity, rather than computed before the work begins.</p>

<p>"Laws of Coding" names the craft layer, defined with the other three layers below. David Charlot's proposal, stated here as conjecture, is that this craft can gain a small set of rules from which the behaviour and the cost of the code it produces can be computed before it runs, by borrowing from the layers beneath it. The candidates are (L1) a deterministic transition rule, (L2) an account of information and energy per transition, (L3) a canonical Form for every program, which is compression, and (L4) a composition rule under which L1 to L3 survive when programs are joined. The <a href="https://research.openie.dev/about/rce/">Reality Code Engine (RCE)</a> proposes candidates for each; hypotheses H1 to H13 are the tests (H11 to H13 are stated in the proposer section above). Nothing in this section is a measured result unless it carries a number and a source.</p>

<p>The analogy has limits, stated here. Newton described a world nobody designed; programs are made by people, so their rules are partly chosen rather than discovered. Mechanics has continuous quantities and exact conservation of energy; a program's state is discrete and finite, and the energy a chip spends depends on the hardware, not only on the program. Rice's theorem [T20] says that no general procedure decides any non-trivial property of what an arbitrary program computes, and the halting problem [T19] is the best-known case; RCE avoids these limits only by working in a restricted language whose programs carry termination bounds, and that restriction has a price in what can be expressed. Finally, even the <em>Principia</em> was not the "clockwork universe": Smith notes that aspect "was added by Laplace" [T1, Sec. 1]; the axioms of motion alone did not settle every problem of mechanics either, since for bodies that are not point-masses they "must be supplemented by further principles" [T1, Sec. 5]; and "Newtonian theory is now seen to hold only to high approximation in limited circumstances" [T1, Sec. 1]. The claim is an analogy, not an equivalence.</p>

<h3 id="not-yet">Four layers of law, from theory to art</h3>

<p>Four phrases are easy to blur, and this page keeps them apart. Each names a different layer, running from theory through science to art, and each is formalized to a different degree today. They are never used interchangeably here.</p>

