<p><strong>How RCE-JEPA relates to the layers.</strong> RCE-JEPA is a bridge, not a merger. It works in the "Laws of Coding" layer, where a learned model proposes code, and it ties each proposal to the layers beneath: the Form has a fixed meaning ("Laws of Programming Languages"), accepted Forms can be transformed by stated rules ("Laws of Programming"), and their energy is accounted against physical limits ("Laws of Computation"). Each hypothesis below names the layer it tests. If the bridge holds, the craft gains rules borrowed from the science and the theory; the layers stay distinct.</p>

<p>Mechanics has its <em>Principia</em>. Computation does not, yet. The pieces of "Laws of Computation" listed above are exact, but they have not been joined into one formal account that predicts behaviour and cost together, the way the <em>Principia</em> joined Kepler's and Galileo's regularities. This is stated as an open problem, not a solved one.</p>

<p>Nothing in that gap is a failure of the people who came before. Each era did what its machines, its theory of information and its craft of building and optimizing programs allowed. The timeline below says what each era had to work with, with figures from the sources named, and what it achieved.</p>

