<h3 id="chain">The provable chain: hypothesis, layer, evidence</h3>

<p>A claim about "Laws of Coding" is only as strong as its weakest link. The table names, for each hypothesis, which of the four layers of law it tests, then follows it through three layers of implementation: the algorithm (the mathematics of the Form and the predictor), the software environment (checker, corpus, harness) and the hardware (the machine and its meter). Each link carries one of three labels. <strong>[C] cited literature:</strong> a published result, read as stated in the ledger. <strong>[M] measured data:</strong> a number measured by the OpenIE authors, with its source. <strong>[P] planned experiment:</strong> a test not yet run. At the time of writing three links carry measured data, all of it the OpenIE authors' own and none of it outside evidence; the chain is a plan for turning [P] into [M], and any [P] can still break it.</p>

