<h3 id="who-holds">Who holds these views and why</h3>

<p>Ideas rarely arrive alone. Each part of the thesis echoes arguments made, for their own reasons, by people who have never heard of RCE. They are listed as convergence observations: agreement in direction, never confirmation of the RCE project's claims. Where the OpenIE authors read a source in full it is quoted with its section; where they did not, the entry says so and quotes nothing.</p>

<p><strong>Those who already think this way.</strong> The idea that programming and computation should have laws of their own did not originate with the RCE project, and it is older than most of the field. This page joins a conversation that has run for at least forty years and lists it as convergence, not priority.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Hoare and colleagues, "Laws of Programming" (1987).</strong> Nine authors argued that "conventional procedural programs are mathematical expressions, and that they are subject to a set of laws as rich and elegant as those of any other branch of mathematics, engineering, or natural science" [T36, p. 672], and gave nearly one hundred of them. They also named the gap that remains (quoted in the timeline above) [T36, p. 686].</li>
  <li><strong>Bennett and Landauer, "The Fundamental Physical Limits of Computation" (1985).</strong> "We are looking for general laws that must govern all information processing, no matter how it is accomplished" [T35, p. 48]. They add a caution this page shares: "To the extent that we have found limits, they are terribly far away from the real limits of modern technology" [T35, p. 48]. Their result for reversible machines: "There is thus no minimum amount of energy that must be expended in order to perform any given computation" [T35, p. 51], while erasure and error correction still dissipate, since, in their words, "Any correctional device must therefore dissipate some energy" [T35, p. 51].</li>
  <li><strong>McKee, "A few (proposed) fundamental laws of computation" (1995).</strong> A short essay in IEEE Computer proposing laws for the field [T42]. The OpenIE authors have not obtained the text, so it is not quoted or summarized beyond its title.</li>
  <li><strong>Carette, James and Sabry, "Embracing the laws of physics" (2022).</strong> They note that quantum theory and thermodynamics "imply that information is a conserved quantity of physical processes and hence of primitive computational operations", and set out "to redevelop foundational computational models in a way that embraces the principle of conservation of information" [T43, abstract, PDF p. 2]. Their closing summary: "The entire edifice of computer science including its mainstream models of computations, programming languages, and logics is founded on classical physics" [T43, Sec. 5.5, p. 45].</li>
  <li><strong>Lehman (1980)</strong> states five empirical regularities of software evolution for programs embedded in the world [T41]. The first, "Continuing Change": a program "that is used and that as an implementation of its specification reflects some other reality, undergoes continual change or becomes progressively less useful" [T41, Table I, p. 1068]. He is explicit that these are not physics: "They are, however, clearly not immutable" in the way physical and chemical regularities are, since they "arise from the habits and practices of people and organizations" [T41, Sec. V-A, p. 1069], the same distance this page keeps between "Laws of Coding" and "Laws of Computation". <strong>Brooks</strong> distinguished "essential tasks, the fashioning of the complex conceptual structures that compose the abstract software entity" from accidental ones [T40, PDF p. 1]. <strong>Dijkstra</strong> observed that a computer spans a ratio of time grains of "10<sup>10</sup> or more", which makes it "the first to provide us with an environment where highly hierarchical artefacts are both possible and necessary" [T37].</li>
</ul>
<p>The groups below each hold one strand of the same idea.</p>


<p><strong>Compression as understanding (T1, T2).</strong> Solomonoff, Kolmogorov and Chaitin defined complexity as shortest description [T3, T4, T21]; Rissanen made it a model selection rule [T6]; Hutter's prize and AIXI and Sutskever's talks extend it to intelligence (not read here; not quoted). Delétang and colleagues at DeepMind argue that "a model that compresses well generalizes well" [T7, Sec. 1] and show large language models compress images and audio. Schmidhuber argues that if the history of the universe were computable, its simplest explanation "would be the shortest program that computes it" [T8, Sec. 1]. Disagreement: Delétang et al. warn that "scaling beyond a certain point will deteriorate the compression performance since the model parameters need to be accounted for" [T7, Sec. 1], and Chollet argues that "solely measuring skill at any given task falls short of measuring intelligence" [T22, abstract]; compression of a fixed corpus is not the same as efficient learning of new skills.</p>

<p><strong>Computation as physics (T3).</strong> Landauer [T9], Bennett [T10, T44] and Lloyd [T11] hold that information processing is a physical process with energy costs and limits. Lloyd: "Energy limits speed. Entropy limits memory." [T11, Sec. 1.5]. Wolpert's stochastic thermodynamics treats computation, including finite automata, as non-equilibrium physics: "One of the most prominent aspects of computers is that they are inherently nonequilibrium systems" [T12, abstract]; finite automata are treated in Sec. XII [T12]. Fredkin and Toffoli's conservative logic [T23], Feynman's lectures on computation, Wheeler's "it from bit" and Wolfram's computational universe point the same way (not read here). Disagreement: Bennett records objections that Landauer's principle is false, or, being a consequence of the second law of thermodynamics, either unnecessary or insufficient to exorcise Maxwell's demon, and answers them [T10]; the debate is about foundations, not about the size of the floor. A second disagreement is historical: Landauer wrote "We believe that devices exhibiting logical irreversibility are essential to computing" [T9, Sec. 3, p. 186], and Bennett's 1973 construction answered that they are not, at the price of about twice as many steps and a large temporary history [T44, pp. 525, 529]; Bennett then estimated "kT ln 2 is, as Landauer has argued [1], an approximate lower bound" only for machines that throw away about one bit per step [T44, p. 532].</p>

<p><strong>State machines and deterministic semantics (T1, T3).</strong> Turing's machines [T19], Milner's calculus [T24], Harel's statecharts [T25], Lamport's TLA, which treats a program as a set of behaviours over states [T26], the synchronous languages Esterel and Lustre (Berry, Halbwachs) [T27, T28], Elliott's functional reactive programming [T29], the Elm architecture, deterministic simulation testing at FoundationDB and TigerBeetle, and Hoare's and Dijkstra's view of programs as predicates [T30] all treat a program as a state machine with exact semantics. They are cited as history; none were read in full for this section, so none are quoted. Limits: Turing [T19] and Rice [T20] bound what can be decided about arbitrary programs; deterministic semantics is easy to state for a closed language and hard to keep across real hardware, threads and networks.</p>

<p><strong>Programs as data on a low-dimensional manifold (T2).</strong> Hindle et al. found software "natural", more repetitive than English [T15] (not read in full here). Alon et al.'s code2vec maps code to vectors where "semantically similar objects are mapped to close vectors" [T16, Sec. 1]. Sketch-based synthesis (Solar-Lezama), DreamCoder's library learning (Ellis et al. 2021, cited in Section 2 below; its supplement describes library learning as "automatic refactoring" [Ellis et al. 2021, supplement S3.2, p. 39]) and Allamanis's survey of machine learning on code point the same way (Solar-Lezama and Allamanis not read here). Disagreement: code2vec's authors report that "When given uninformative, obfuscated or adversarial variable names, the prediction of the label is usually less accurate" [T16, Sec. 7]: learned embeddings can track names rather than meaning, which is the gap a canonical Form is meant to close.</p>

<p><strong>World models and latent prediction (T4).</strong> LeCun's JEPA programme at Meta FAIR (cited above), Ha and Schmidhuber, who "learn a compressed spatial and temporal representation of the environment" [T14, abstract], MuZero [T13], physics-informed neural networks, where prior physical knowledge "can act as a regularization agent that constrains the space of admissible solutions to a manageable size" [T31, Sec. 1], and Hamiltonian neural networks, motivated by models whose simulation "drifts over time to higher or lower energy states" [T32, Sec. 1], all build structure into learned dynamics. Lagrangian networks (Cranmer), Dreamer (Hafner) and Koopman operator learning share the aim (not read here). Disagreement: Ha and Schmidhuber found that a learned world model "will be exploitable by the controller, even if in the actual environment such exploits do not exist" [T14, Sec. 4.5]; Greydanus et al. note their method assumes a conserved quantity and so cannot model friction [T32, Sec. 3.2]. For code, the checker is the guard against exploiting the model's errors.</p>

<p><strong>Energy-based models (T4).</strong> Hopfield [T33] and Hinton [T34] framed learning as shaping an energy surface (not read here); Du and Mordatch [T17] show implicit generation by descending a learned energy, and LeCun (cited above) places energy minimization at the centre of JEPA inference. Disagreement within the field concerns training stability and sampling cost, which Du and Mordatch discuss [T17].</p>

